AUDIO: 'whatever it was before. And a lot of you told us the same thing about the home screen. The left-hand side was wasted dead space. Digital tumbleweed.'

[05:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, we fixed it. But we didn\'t want to just cram it full of stuff for the sake of it. We wanted to add functionality that actually earns its place on the screen. So, imagine this.'

[05:23] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DB Pro application showing a "Downloading Update" progress bar.
AUDIO: 'What if you could pin your favorite things? For example, that dashboard you check every morning. A connection, a workflow, or a saved query. You can pin them all right there,'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one click away the moment DB Pro boots up. And now, my favorite feature of this whole release. In 2.3, we killed the database password.'

[05:48] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DB Pro UI showing a dashboard with various data panels.
AUDIO: 'Okay, fine. We made it optional, but we made it optional as a much worse thumbnail. If you\'re connecting to a managed database, you\'ve probably got a'

[06:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'long-lived password sitting in a config file somewhere. It\'s been there for months. The fix is identity-based short-lived token auth instead of storing a static password. So, DB Pro generates a fresh'

[06:13] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The new DB Pro Home Screen with pinned and recent connections, workflows, and saved queries.
AUDIO: 'token every time you connect using the cloud entity you already have. We shipped four flavors: AWS RDS, IAM,'

[06:26] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DB Pro connection settings, showing "User & Password" authentication.
AUDIO: 'Azure AD, Google Cloud Platform SQL IAM, and MongoDB Atlas workload identity. That covers all the big managed providers, Postgres, MySQL, and'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, that\'s Devlog 10. We had a new logo, a new website, new home screen, workflows, and passwordless cloud identity auth.'

